  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to use a gradient-free optimization method with careful exploitation of the problem structure to minimize the AC1 upper bound. This method integrates:\n- Structured initialization based on parent sequences and random templates\n- Adaptive dimensionality and convective updates\n- Linear programming-based updates to enforce constraints\n- A two-phase approach: initialization and guided search\n\nThe method leverages the fact that the AC1 upper bound is a convex function of the convolution outputs, making it suitable for linearization constraints. The primary advantage is avoiding problematic local minima while ensuring the search stays within bounds.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: a = [a\u2080, a\u2081, ..., a_{n-1}] \u2208 [0, 1000]^n\n- Normalization: a = b_emb / ||b_emb||_2, ensuring unit length for gradients\n- Objective: f(a) = (2*n*max_conv(a)) / ||a||_2\u00b2, minimized by lerp updates\n- Constraints:\n  - a_i \u2265 0 for all i (linear constraints)\n  - ||a||_2\u00b2 \u2265 0.01 (scaling formulation)\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Phase 0: Initialization\n  - Use parent sequence if available, normalize with clipping\n  - Generate random templates:\n    - Uniform: np.ones(n)/n\n    - Exponential: np.exp(rng.standard_normal(n))\n    - Sine: np.sin(rng.uniform(-\u03c0, \u03c0, n))\n- Phase 1: Adam optimization\n  - State: {theta: n, b: uniform(n), layers: num_conv, tau: initial_scale}\n  - Iterate until budget exhaustion:\n    - Evaluate \u03b8 = np.dot(b, b)\n    - Build Hessian \u2248 I + \u03c1\u00b7diag(random walks)\n    - Project updates to enforce non-negativity\n    - Update b by AdaMax if \u03b8 > 0.01\n- Phase 2: Linear projection updates\n  - Create binary pulse trains based on round(theta\u00b7x)\n  - Use projected gradient method with ||x||_\u221e constraint\n- Phase 3: Final selection\n  - Keep best candidate and clip to [0, 1000]\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Default parameters:\n  - n: 64, 128, 256, 512 dimensions\n  - tau: 0.001 (initial scale)\n  - lr: 0.01 (learning rate)\n  - batch: 8 (gradient steps)\n- Variations:\n  - Binary chop: halve dimension if low candidates\n  - Random projection: projection matrix +ReLU nonlinearity\n  - Batched Adam: for larger dimensions 1024+\n  - Warm restarts: periodic resetting helps escape traps\n\n5. Budget and estimation\n- Time estimation:\n  - Adam: O(n\u00b2 log(budget)) per optimization\n  - LP updates: O(n\u00b3) per round\n- Schedule:\n  - Check deadlines inside LP calls\n  - 30s for Adam, 60s for LP\n-fallback: if early no improvement, return parent template\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Invariants checked:\n  - all(a_i \u2265 0)\n  - sum(b) \u2265 0.01\n  - no reruns of LP/LP without cost accounting\n- Returns only valid candidates, first exact SC computed via eval_sequence",
